@@ -294,6 +294,61 @@ extension on [`IDataContext`](xref:NextORM.Core.IDataContext), so it works wheth
 interface. Independently of entities, [`From`](xref:NextORM.Core.DataContextExtensions.From(NextORM.Core.IDataContext,System.String)) can also wrap a subquery
 (`dataContext.From(innerQuery)`) or another entity builder (`dataContext.From(entity)`).
 
+## Dynamic columns (runtime schema)
+
+A mapped entity can expose the table's "extra" columns - those that have no CLR property - through a
+single dictionary. Mark an `IDictionary<string, object?>` property with
+[`DynamicColumnsAttribute`](xref:NextORM.Core.DynamicColumnsAttribute) or the fluent
+[`DynamicColumns()`](xref:NextORM.Core.EntityPropertyBuilder`1.DynamicColumns):
+
+```csharp
+[SqlTable("events")]
+public class EventRow
+{
+    [Key] public int Id { get; set; }
+    public string? Name { get; set; }
+    [DynamicColumns] public IDictionary<string, object?> Extra { get; set; } = new Dictionary<string, object?>();
+}
+```
+
+The store is not a physical column: it is never projected by itself and is excluded from the mapped
+property set (read it as [`IEntityMetadata.DynamicColumnsStore`](xref:NextORM.Core.IEntityMetadata.DynamicColumnsStore)
+and [`IPropertyMetadata.IsDynamicColumnsStore`](xref:NextORM.Core.IPropertyMetadata.IsDynamicColumnsStore)).
+
+To read the runtime columns, list them on the entity query with
+[`WithDynamicColumns`](xref:NextORM.Core.EntityBuilder`1.WithDynamicColumns(System.String[])); the names
+are discovered from the schema at run time (for example from `INFORMATION_SCHEMA`). The set is part of
+the plan key, so two queries with different sets never share a cached plan. `WithDynamicColumns` applies
+only to an entity query without an explicit `Select` projection.
+
+```csharp
+var row = dataContext.From<EventRow>()
+    .Where(x => x.Id == 1)
+    .WithDynamicColumns("region", "score")
+    .First();
+// row.Extra["region"], row.Extra["score"]
+```
+
+The identifiers are always quoted with the provider's delimiter (`"..."` on PostgreSQL/SQLite, `[...]`
+on SQL Server, `` `...` `` on MySQL/MariaDB/ClickHouse), so a runtime name cannot inject SQL - still only
+pass names from a trusted source.
+
+On write the keys of the store are rendered as columns by `INSERT`, `UPDATE` and `MERGE`:
+
+```csharp
+await dataContext.InsertInto<EventRow>().Values(new EventRow
+{
+    Id = 1, Name = "n",
+    Extra = new Dictionary<string, object?> { ["region"] = "eu", ["score"] = 5 },
+}).InsertAsync();
+```
+
+A key that collides with a declared column is ignored (the declared property wins); for a multi-row
+write the column set is the union of the rows' keys and a row missing a key writes `NULL`.
+
+The in-memory provider has no columnar row, so it returns the source entity's own store unchanged. Bulk
+insert does not write dynamic columns.
+
 ## Quoted identifiers
 
 By default table and column names are emitted verbatim (see [Provider differences](#provider-differences)),
